===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|physiology}} The act or process of [[swallowing#Noun|swallowing]]. {{defdate|from mid 17th c.}}
#: {{synonyms|en|glutition}}
#* {{quote-book|title=A View of the People of the Whole World|year=1654|passage={{...}} the action of the gullet, that is '''deglutition''', is performed and consummated, the action being animall and partly Naturall.|author=John Bulwer|oclc=559247647|authorlink=John Bulwer}}
#* {{quote-book|author=[Herman] Boerhaave|authorlink=Herman Boerhaave|chapter=Of the Origin, Nature, and Mixture of the ''Saliva'' with the Aliments|title=Dr. Boerhaave’s Academical Lectures on the Theory of Physic.{{nb...|Being a Genuine Translation of His Institutews and Explanatory Comment, Collated and Adjusted to Each Other, as They were Dictated to His Students at the University of Leyden.}}|location=London|publisher=Printed for W Innys,{{nb...|at the West End of St. Paul’s.}}|year=1742|volume=I (Containing the History of Physic,{{nb...|and the Oeconomy of the Several Parts Subservient to Chylification}})|section=§&nbsp;70|page=170|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=eVATq4FUq1gC&pg=PA170|oclc=938339039|passage=The ''Uvula'' {{...}} ſerves as an arched and ſlippery Bridge, exactly fitted every way to cover the convex Sides and ''Rima'' of the ''Glottis'', for the Aliment to ſlide eaſily along in '''Deglutition'''; it prevents the Aliment from falling out of the ''Fauces'' into the ''Larynx'' in '''Deglutition'''; and being moved by its Muſcles, protruds the Aliment backward into the upper Part of the ''Fauces''.}}
#* {{RQ:Charlotte Bronte Jane Eyre|volume=II|chapter=VI|page=174|passage=Feeling without judgment is a washy draught indeed; but judgment untempered by feeling is too bitter and husky a morsel for human '''deglutition'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=W[illiam] H[enry] Hudson|authorlink=William Henry Hudson|chapter=The Village and ‘The Stones’|title=Afoot in England|location=London|publisher=[[w:Hutchinson (publisher)|Hutchinson & Co.]]{{nb...|Paternoster Row}}|year=1909|page=251|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/afootinengland00hudsgoog#page/n265/mode/1up|oclc=31584816|passage=Some philosopher has said that the chief pleasure in a man's life, as in that of a cow, consists in the processes of mastication, '''deglutition''', and digestion, and I am very much inclined to agree with him.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Gerard J. Tortora|author2=Bryan Derrickson|chapter=The Digestive System|title=Principles of Anatomy and Physiology|edition=12th|location=Hoboken, N.J.|publisher=[[w:Wiley (publisher)|Wiley]]|year=2008|page=935|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=uNwfOPPYgKAC&pg=PA935|column=2|isbn=978-0-470-08471-7|passage=The movement of food from the mouth into the stomach is achieved by the act of swallowing, or '''deglutition'''{{nb...}}. '''Deglutition''' is facilitated by the secretion of saliva and mucus and involves the mouth, pharynx, and esophagus.}}

